  "title": "The Power/Root Combo",
  "courseId": "radicals-and-exponents",
  "chapterId": "ch3-rational-exponents",
  "minutes": 11,
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "c1",
      "kind": "concept",
      "figure": "rad-read-fraction",
      "body": "A^(m/n) means take the n-th root, then raise to the m-th power: a^(m/n) = (ⁿ√a)^m. For 8^(2/3): ∛8 = 2, then 2² = 4. Take the root first — it keeps the numbers small — then apply the power."
    },
    {
      "id": "i1",
      "kind": "interactive",
      "body": "Root then power.",
      "widget": {
        "type": "expLogExplore",
        "mode": "exponential",
        "x": 0.667,
        "targetBase": 8,
        "startBase": 3,
        "showMirror": false,
        "prompt": "Slide the base until base^(2/3) reads 4.",
        "successFeedback": "Base 8: 8^(2/3) = (8^(1/3))² = 2² = 4. The fractional exponent does two jobs at once — the denominator (3) roots, the numerator (2) then powers — and both happen to this one base.",
        "lowFeedback": "base^(2/3) is still under 4.",
        "highFeedback": "base^(2/3) has passed 4."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "k1",
      "kind": "check",
      "body": "Fourth root, cubed.",
      "conceptTag": "rad-mn-exp",
      "explanationVariants": [
        "⁴√16 = 2, then 2³ = 8.",
        "16^(3/4) = (⁴√16)³ = 2³ = 8."
      ],
      "widget": {
        "type": "exactNumberLab",
        "prompt": "What is 16^(3/4)?",
        "task": "rationalExponentEvaluate",
        "values": [
          {
