    {
      "id": "c2",
      "kind": "concept",
      "body": "Taking the root first keeps the arithmetic easy. 27^(2/3) = (∛27)² = 3² = 9, much simpler than 27² = 729 and then cube-rooting. Same answer, far less work.",
      "figure": "rad-power-root"
    },
    {
      "id": "i2",
      "kind": "interactive",
      "body": "A number riddle.",
      "widget": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "prompt": "I am 64^(2/3). What number am I?",
        "answer": 16,
        "tolerance": 0,
        "commonErrors": [
          {
            "value": 4,
            "feedback": "That's just ∛64. Apply the power 2: 4² = 16."
          },
          {
            "value": 43,
            "feedback": "Don't multiply 64 by 2/3. Take the cube root (4), then square it: 16."
          }
        ],
        "fallbackFeedback": "64^(2/3) = (∛64)² = 4² = 16."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "c3",
      "kind": "concept",
      "body": "Read the fraction as instructions: the **numerator is the power**, the **denominator is the root**. For 32^(2/5): the fifth root of 32 is 2, and 2² = 4. Denominator down (root), numerator up (power).",
      "figure": "rad-read-fraction"
    },
    {
      "id": "i3",
      "kind": "interactive",
      "body": "Solve it step by step.",
      "widget": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "prompt": "What is the value of 32^(3/5)? (Take the fifth root of 32, then cube the result.)",
        "answer": 8,
        "tolerance": 0,
        "commonErrors": [
          {
            "value": 2,
            "feedback": "That's just the fifth root. Apply the power 3: 2³ = 8."
          },
          {
            "value": 19,
            "feedback": "Don't multiply 32 by 3/5. Take the fifth root (2), then cube it: 8."
          }
        ],
        "fallbackFeedback": "32^(3/5) = (⁵√32)³ = 2³ = 8."
      }
    },
